% openshell-assets: the six assets of the lecture, each with the OpenShell layer that guards it and the ATLAS technique
% that acts on it. Replaces the tables "OpenShell controls by protected asset" and "Limits of OpenShell enforcement".
% Canvas 16.5 x 5.0 cm.  Render: ./render.sh openshell-assets 6   (needs standalone-style.tex from the same folder)
% Six columns, 2.7 cm apart, centred at x = 1.4, 4.1, 6.8, 9.5, 12.2, 14.9. In each: the asset (gnode, y 4.6), a flow arrow
% down to the OpenShell layer that guards it (rnode, y 2.6), and the technique under it (note, top at y 1.2); all three
% are drawn lower (assets at 4.2, layers at 2.2, techniques from 0.95 down) so the canvas carries no empty band under them.
% The two assets that OpenShell does not guard (definition text and response) have a ghost box that gives the reason;
% its text is deckGrey rather than the style's 35 percent grey so the reason stays legible, as in lecture 5's exfiltration figure.
% Every box opens and closes with a strut, so sibling boxes with the same number of lines share their baselines.
% The newest asset is outlined in navy at 1.6 pt on its own stage and settles to the plain outline on the next.
% Stage 1 definition text: none, text reaches the model; Definition poisoning.
% Stage 2 implementation: filesystem policy, read-only code; Implementation poisoning.
% Stage 3 response: none, text reaches the model; Runtime-response poisoning.
% Stage 4 stored credential: provider attachment, placeholder, not the key; Credentials from configuration.
% Stage 5 outbound connection: supervisor, network policy, L7 enforce; Exfiltration via tool invocation.
% Stage 6 kernel boundary: Landlock and seccomp baseline; Escape to Host.
% Sources: the two tables of the lecture (Rahul's save of 2026-10-15, slides 104 to 114) and OpenShell's own documentation
% at commit be7af99 (/Users/rkrsn/workspace/imputed/openshell, the commit the HW2 spec cites):
%   provider attachment: docs/security/best-practices.mdx, "The agent receives an opaque placeholder, not the real API key or
%     access token", resolved only for the hosts, ports and paths the provider profile authorizes (docs/about/overview.mdx);
%   supervisor, network policy, L7 enforce: docs/about/architecture.mdx (the supervisor "checks requests against policy,
%     supplies credentials ... opens approved connections"); docs/how-it-works/policies/network-rules.mdx ("enforce blocks
%     the request", audit "is the default");
%   Landlock and seccomp baseline: docs/security/best-practices.mdx ("The sandbox installs a mandatory Landlock baseline");
%     its seccomp filters block the syscalls that "support container escape (mount, pivot_root, move_mount, namespace
%     creation)" and "raw kernel bypass (bpf, io_uring_setup, perf_event_open)";
%   filesystem policy, read-only code: docs/how-it-works/policies/schema.mdx (read_only, read_write), with the HW2 policy's
%     read_only grant on the target's code.
% Techniques are the lecture's short names (its tables and notes) for ATLAS release 2026.09 entries: AI Agent Tool Poisoning:
% Definition and Instructions (AML.T0110.000), : Implementation (AML.T0110.001), : Runtime Response (AML.T0110.002);
% Credentials from AI Agent Configuration (AML.T0083); Exfiltration via AI Agent Tool Invocation (AML.T0086); Escape to Host
% (AML.T0105), the last one exact. Checked in lecture-5--2026-10-07/slides/data/atlas-2026.09.yaml.
% Left out on purpose: the tables' rows for agentic resource consumption, rug pull and
% supply chain, and context poisoning, which are not among the lecture's six assets, and the ATLAS mitigation column.
% No magenta: nothing here is drawn as content the adversary controls. Technique names only, no identifiers.
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \canvas{16.5}{5.0}
  \tikzset{
    words/.style={execute at begin node={\hyphenpenalty=10000\exhyphenpenalty=10000\frenchspacing\strut},  % break at spaces only;
                 execute at end node={\strut}},                                                  % struts: shared baselines
    asset/.style={gnode, words, text width=2.3cm, inner xsep=2pt, minimum height=11mm},
    newasset/.style={asset, draw=deckNavy, line width=1.6pt},
    guard/.style={rnode, words, font=\sffamily\footnotesize, text width=2.3cm, inner xsep=2pt, minimum height=17mm},
    noguard/.style={ghost, words, font=\sffamily\footnotesize, text width=2.3cm, inner xsep=2pt, minimum height=17mm,
                    draw=ibmfg!50, line width=0.6pt, text=deckGrey},
    tech/.style={note, words, anchor=north, align=center, text width=2.4cm, inner sep=0pt},
  }
  % \column{k}{x}{asset}{guard style}{guard text}{technique}
  \newcommand{\column}[6]{%
    \atstage{#1}{
      \ifnum\stage=#1 \node[newasset] (a#1) at (#2,4.2) {#3};\else \node[asset] (a#1) at (#2,4.2) {#3};\fi
      \node[#4] (g#1) at (#2,2.2) {#5};
      \draw[flow] (a#1.south) -- (g#1.north);
      \node[tech] at (#2,0.95) {#6};
    }}
  \column{1}{1.4}{definition text}{noguard}{none:\\text reaches\\the model}{Definition poisoning}
  \column{2}{4.1}{implementation}{guard}{filesystem policy, read-only code}{Implementation poisoning}
  \column{3}{6.8}{response}{noguard}{none:\\text reaches\\the model}{Runtime-response poisoning}
  \column{4}{9.5}{stored credential}{guard}{provider attachment: placeholder, not the key}{Credentials from configuration}
  \column{5}{12.2}{outbound connection}{guard}{supervisor: network policy, L7 enforce}{Exfiltration via tool invocation}
  \column{6}{14.9}{kernel boundary}{guard}{Landlock and seccomp baseline}{Escape to Host}
\end{tikzpicture}
